{"description":"The ADR-035 capture envelope was declared as literals in four places at once: one `if v, ok := meta[...]` block per field inside the shared conformance pass, one entry per field in the JSON allowlist, one field per struct on `exchange.Unit` and `conformance.Artifact`, and one assignment per field in the exchange projection and both renderers. Nothing tied the copies together, which is how `provenance` came to be carried by the unit, validated by conformance, and never written by the renderer for months: each copy was individually defensible and no test compared them. Measured market data points the same way — GitClear's 623M-change study reports code-block duplication up 81% and refactoring down 70% as AI authorship scaled — so this is a structural countermeasure, not tidying.","acceptanceCriteria":"- One declarative table (`envelopeFields`) states every envelope field: metadata key, kind, paired unit flag, and where a validated value lands.\n- The conformance validation pass iterates the table; one arm per kind owns the shape check, so two adapters cannot disagree about a shape.\n- `allowedJSONTopLevel` is built from a literal base plus the table's keys — an envelope key is never restated in the allowlist.\n- `captureMetaKeys` is derived from the nested table.\n- A reflection test fails when a table entry has no matching field on `exchange.Unit`/`conformance.Artifact`, or when either model grows an envelope field with no table entry (non-envelope fields listed explicitly as a baseline).\n- Every pinned diagnostic message is unchanged: `conformance/envelope_test.go` passes without an edit.","debtRationale":""}